straight alpha is not premultiplied alpha, and PNG holds the first one

O2argb8u is premultiplied throughout. The source-over span in O2argb8u.m is

  Cr = Cs + Cd * (255 - As) / 255

with no multiply by As, so a straight sample pushed through it comes out too bright wherever alpha is
partial. Everything below follows from that one equation.

THREE THINGS WERE WRONG AND THEY COMPOUNDED.

kO2ImageAlphaLast and kO2ImageAlphaPremultipliedLast SHARED A READER. The two labels therefore meant
the same pixels, and any image that honestly declared straight alpha was composited as though it were
already multiplied. The icns decoder is exactly such an image, so the modern icon types decoded
correctly and then drew too bright.

The PNG decoder stored NEITHER FORMAT. It clamped each colour to the alpha,

  pixels[i] = MIN(r, a)

and labelled the result premultiplied. That is right only where the sample is at or above the alpha:
a half transparent white, r=255 a=128, clamps to 128 and happens to be correct, while r=100 a=128
stays 100 where premultiplying gives 50. So the data was lossy in one direction and untouched in the
other.

And the label was wrong against macOS. PNG has no premultiplied variant, and Core Graphics reports
kCGImageAlphaLast for the same file.

THE FIX PUTS THE MULTIPLY WHERE IT BELONGS. Two new readers premultiply on the way into O2argb8u,
kO2ImageAlphaLast gets its own case pointing at them, the clamp loop is gone, and the PNG decoder
says kO2ImageAlphaLast because that is what it holds.

MEASURED. The ImageIO icns case in the darling testsuite goes from failing on the alpha attribute to
EXIT 0, and the batch goes from 63 passed 2 failed to 64 passed 1 failed, the remaining one being
PubSub and unrelated.

AND LOOKED AT, because a premultiplication error shows up as haloing or darkened edges rather than as
a crash. All five roster applications were driven and every capture examined: LibreOffice, whose
toolbar is the most transparent-PNG-dense surface on the roster, has clean icon edges; Swift
Publisher renders its template thumbnails in full colour; MoneyMoney, iTerm2 and iA Writer are
unchanged. No haloing, no dark fringing.

